Without /proc, holder_alive (bin/pidly:141-153) falls back to kill -0 and
ignores the recorded start time, so on macOS and the BSDs a reused pid
looks like a live claim — the case the pid.starttime scheme exists to
catch. `ps -o lstart= -p PID` or similar could restore it; at the least,
document the weaker guarantee in the README.


Done 2026-11-04: without /proc, starttime reads ps -o lstart= as YYYYMMDDhhmmss and holder_alive compares it, so a recycled pid reads dead there too. Checked by running a copy of the script with the /proc branches disabled: a live claim reads live, the same pid with another start time reads dead.
